KeyFacts is a measurement strip of three to six figures, each with its unit and the standard behind it, that tells an engineer what a page stands on before they read a word of copy.

- Markup: `<dl class="aq-key-facts" data-theme="light">` with one `<div>` per fact: `<dt>` (`<span class="aq-key-facts__i" aria-hidden="true">01 / </span>` and the parameter, "Conductivity at 25 °C"), then `<dd class="aq-key-facts__value">` (comparator `aq-key-facts__cmp`, figure `aq-key-facts__fig`, unit `aq-key-facts__unit`; `is-long` when the value is words or a placeholder), an optional second `<dd class="aq-key-facts__src">` (the standard, "USP <645>"), then `<span class="aq-key-facts__dial" aria-hidden="true">`. Keep `dt` first so screen readers read the name, then the value, then the source.
- On the site, `wKeyFacts(facts)` in `assets/src/parts/KeyFacts.js` builds it from page `facts` `{l, v}`: the label splits at the last " · " into name and source; the value splits off a leading comparator and a trailing unit (only when the tail after the last space has no digits and follows a figure, so "Part 11" and "[Ra, µm]" stay whole). Placeholders keep their `[brackets]`.
- Anatomy (concept 39): a cream data plate (`data-theme="light"`, so it reads as a plate on the navy hero) with a double `line-strong` frame, tick rulers inside its top and bottom edge (minor every `space-2`, major every `space-16`), `line-strong` dividers with round fixings where they meet the rulers. Each cell: "01 / NAME" in 12 px mono caps over a hairline; the figure centred, Geist Mono 400 in `ink`, 34 to 52 px (26 to 38 px from five facts on; 20 to 26 px when `is-long`), wrapping only at spaces; the unit under it in `flow` mono caps; a small dial (decoration) beside it; the source in 12 px mono over a dashed rule at the foot.
- Phones: one fact per row, the name full width on top (12 px), then the figure left (28 px; 17 px when long), the source and dial right; no fixings.
- Value split: a leading number keeps a short unit tail whole ("10 CFU/100 mL" -> "10" + "CFU/100 mL"); "long" (smaller type) is judged on the figure alone, so a short number with a long unit stays large.
- Index, dial, rulers and fixings plot nothing.
- On the site it sits at the bottom of the Hero (`facts` in `content/pages/`). Take every figure from the page's own spec table or copy; unconfirmed figures stay in `[square brackets]`. Write values exactly as the standard does, with the comparison sign.
- Not for one figure with an explanation: use Readout. Not for full specifications: use SpecTable.
